  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1127 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nFairly quiet across the region late this evening as earlier \nshowers and storms had largely come to an end. However, we do \nexpect storm re-development overnight as shortwave energy over \nwestern NE approaches and low-level moisture transport ramps up \nand points into the area. In addition, we'll have a decent \namount of instability (1000-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE) along with \nprecipitable water values exceeding 2 inches and warm cloud \ndepths over 4.5 km. Therefore, any storms that do develop will \nbe very efficient rain producers, as we saw during the day in \nparts of the area where we observed 2+ inch per hour rainfall \nrates. If we get repeated rounds over any areas, localized \nflooding would be possible, especially in areas that saw rain \nduring the day on Wednesday. Still some questions on how \nwidespread these storms will be, with short term guidance \nranging from a few somewhat short-lived spotty showers to a \nrelatively persistent, solid line. On top of that, even run-to-\nrun consistency is somewhat low. All that said, just given the \nsetup with the aforementioned instability, moisture transport, \nand incoming shortwave energy, tend to lean toward maybe the \nhigher end of shower and storm coverage into Thursday morning, \nso will maintain the 40- 60% chances.\n\nAttention then turns to how long the precip and cloud-cover \nlingers into the day on Thursday as guidance is in good \nagreement of a cold front firing some storms along the NE/SD \nborder by the afternoon and pushing them into northeast NE and \neventually across the area during the evening into overnight \nhours. Should early-day storms and clouds linger longer, \nobviously the incoming line will have less instability to work \nwith and would be a little weaker, and vice versa. Shear also \nlooks to be somewhat across our area (30-35 kts in the 0-6 km \nlayer and 15-25 kts in the 0-3 km layer), so tend to the line \nwill likely become outflow dominant and weaken as it pushes \nthrough. Still, guidance shows pockets of 45-60+ mph winds, \nespecially farther west where 0-3 km shear and instability will \nbe enhanced. Additionally, the environment for efficient rain \nproducing storms will remain in place, so could see some heavy \nrain and localized flooding, especially if any areas see \ntraining storms. \n\nThink the primary severe weather threat should exit our area \ntoward 4-5 AM with perhaps some lingering showers and non-severe\nstorms on the back side of the shortwave and a surface low off \nto our east. The northwesterly flow and lingering precip/clouds \nwill lead to a cooler day with highs in the mid 70s to lower \n80s.\n\nWe'll warm back up a bit for the weekend with upper level \nridging building back in and surface high pressure dominating to\nkeep us dry. Expect lower to mid 80s on Saturday followed by \nmid 80s to lower 90s on Sunday and Monday as southerly flow \nstrengthens on the back side of departing high pressure. Our \nnext rain chances look to arrive by Monday night/Tuesday as a \nlarge scale trough slides by to our north and a cold front \npushes south toward or through the area. Still some spread in \nvarious ensemble members on just how far south the front and \nprecip will make it, but currently seeing about 20-40% chance at\ntimes through Wednesday night, with model consensus keeping \ntemperatures in the 80s to lower 90s.",
